  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAnother night, another round of fog as sfc high pressure remains off \nto our east. South to southeast low level flow will advect moisture \noff Lake Huron, resulting in the best chance for fog near Saginaw \nBay. Seems it could travel inland as well across the interior, and \nleak into eastern upper as well. Patchy fog possible elsewhere. \nOther than that, southerly flow and increasing heights on Saturday \nwill lead to rising temperatures, and the start of a truly nice \nweekend for the end of October.\n\nAbove normal heights aloft, as has been the case, will remain over \nnorthern MI this weekend with southerly transport winds. \nConsequently, temperatures rise into the 60s to near 70 degrees each \nafternoon this weekend. Ridging gets beat down some by impressive \nupper trough across Ontario, and subsequent boundary will leak down \ninto northern MI. Thus, as the energy to the north continues east, \nthe cold front and cooler airmass slowly push into northern MI early \nnext week. This feature will produce showers as well, \nparticularly on Sunday and across eastern Upper Michigan, but\nleakage down into northern lower possible as well (light rain\nfor those \"lucky\" enough). That being said, this cool down will\nfeel cooler than it really is, with progged high temps hovering\naround normal for this time of year (upper 40s to lower 50s \nmost areas). \n\nPretty energetic from Tuesday and through the rest of the week as \nmultiple short waves/upper lows track from the Pac NW/Desert SW across\nthe Upper Midwest. Meanwhile, the northern stream remains \nactive as well. How these features interact and how far south \nany northern stream piece of energy digs will tell us how cool, \nor warm, it may get. Current ensemble guidance means are \ngenerally more warm (or above normal) than cool (or below \nnormal) as the vast majority of guidance keeps the main \ntroughing and subsequent seasonably cold air to the north. \nAdditionally, light precipitation chances are possible, starting\nTuesday into mid week as an upper low tracks into the Upper \nMidwest. How far west this feature tracks will determine how \nmuch precip we get. Farther west and we lose the influence, \nfarther east, and more moisture and synoptic forcing produce \nmore in the way of scattered showers. Latest GEFS prob for 24 \nhour precip >0.1\" is generally low (10-40%) with higher probs \nacross Upper Michigan, perhaps indicating that a farther west \ntrajectory, at least initially, may be the preferred solution.",
